  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Showers likely with possible thunderstorms late\nTuesday afternoon and Tuesday evening over Northern Maine.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nConfidence continues to increase in a quick moving cold front\nmoving across the region Tuesday afternoon into Tuesday night.\nHigh-res models continue to show solid agreement with the \nmajority of showers sticking to the northern areas of the \nstate. Hi-res upper air model soundings indicate instability \nwith more bulk shear driven thunderstorm development possible. \nWeak CAPE and mid level lapse rates should dampen the \ndevelopment of severe thunderstorms but cannot rule out a few\nstronger storms with some small hail and gusty winds. \nThunderstorm potential will decrease towards sunset due the the \nlose of daytime heating.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nCooler than normal temperatures are expected much of the period \nright through next weekend along with the chance for showers. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nAn unsettled weather pattern is expected mid week right through\nnext weekend as upper level troughing persists across northern \nNew England. This will result in temperatures remaining \ngenerally below normal for this time of year, with the exception\nof Wednesday, when high temperatures are actually expected to \nbe above normal. With the upper trough and cold air aloft \nacross the area, there will be the chance for a few showers from\ntime to time, especially during the afternoon hours with \ndiurnal instabilty.",
